(window.HALCYON_MOCK=window.HALCYON_MOCK||[]).push({ part:`Process`,
// case studies for the whole mock (items in any part point at one with case:`id`, cq:n is the item's place in it; see mock/README.md)
cases:[
  { id:`m1c1`, title:`Case: the clinic scheduling rollout`, text:[
    `A regional health network is replacing the scheduling system in its 12 clinics. A vendor supplies the scheduling platform under a fixed price contract. An internal team builds the links to the network's records system in two week sprints. The clinics go live in three waves, on dates set in the network's plan.`,
    `It's the end of sprint 6. The integration backlog has 150 points left, and the team's velocity over the last four sprints was 28, 31, 30, and 31. Wave 1, three clinics, goes live in six weeks.`,
    `The vendor's latest release failed 4 of its 20 acceptance tests. The contract says the vendor fixes failed tests within 10 business days at its own cost.`,
    `The managers of the wave 1 clinics found out their go live date from a network wide email, and one of them has asked for a meeting. In a wave 2 clinic, the nurse manager has told staff the new system will add work and is urging them to keep paper backups.`,
    `To test the links, the team wants a copy of real patient records loaded into its test environment next sprint.`] },
  { id:`m1c2`, title:`Case: the county bridge replacement`, text:[
    `A county is replacing a two lane bridge under a predictive plan. The budget at completion is $12 million over 18 months. A steel supplier works under a fixed price contract, and a general contractor does the site work.`,
    `At the end of month 8, planned value is $6.0 million, earned value is $5.4 million, and actual cost is $6.0 million. The overrun comes from labor rates that are expected to stay where they are.`,
    `The power company must move a transmission line before the girders can be set in month 11. The move was due to finish last week and hasn't started. The power company isn't a party to any of the project's contracts.`,
    `Last week an inspector found two welds out of specification. The contractor ground them out and rewelded them, and they passed reinspection.`,
    `The downtown business association has asked for a temporary footbridge so shoppers can cross during construction. The sponsor wants the monthly report to the county board to show green, because the board votes on next year's budget soon.`] }
],
questions:[

{ q:`A company has publicly committed to cut its carbon emissions by 30 percent. The plan for a new warehouse covers scope, cost, and schedule but doesn't mention emissions. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Leave emissions to the facilities team after handover`,
        `Report the building's emissions once it's running`,
        `Write emissions targets into the project plan`,
        `Ask the sponsor whether the pledge applies here`],
  a:2, why:`Sustainability goals belong in the plan from the start as critical information requirements: what's measured, the targets, who reports, and when. Asking the sponsor is the near miss. Checking scope with the sponsor is reasonable, but a public company commitment already applies, and the question only delays building it in. Leaving it to facilities or reporting after the fact means the design choices that drive emissions get made without the data.`,
  task:`R1`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`An agile team has a product roadmap, a prioritized backlog, and a release plan. A new sponsor asks where the project management plan is. What is the best response?`,
  opts:[`Write a full predictive plan with a detailed schedule for the sponsor`,
        `Walk the sponsor through the roadmap and backlog at a review`,
        `Ask the PMO to excuse the project from its planning requirements`,
        `Give the sponsor a short plan that references the existing artifacts`],
  a:3, why:`Agile projects still plan, just differently. A light plan that points to the roadmap, backlog and release plan meets governance without duplicating anything. A walkthrough is the near miss. It helps the sponsor understand, but it leaves no plan of record. A detailed predictive schedule fights the approach, and an exemption dodges governance.`,
  task:`R1`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`During planning, the project manager finds that the quality management plan requires a two-week inspection that the draft schedule does not include. What should the project manager do next?`,
  opts:[`Work with the plan owners to resolve the conflict before baselining`,
        `Schedule the inspection in parallel with the next activities`,
        `Let the team resolve the conflict during execution`,
        `Baseline the schedule now and add the inspection by change request`],
  a:0, why:`Integrating plans means making the subsidiary plans agree before the baseline is set, so the PM resolves it with the plan owners now. A change request later is the near miss. Change control is a real process, but using it for a conflict you already know about bakes a wrong baseline in on purpose. Running the inspection in parallel may break its logic, and leaving it for execution leaves the conflict open.`,
  task:`R1`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`On a hybrid project, a team member asks how a proposed change to the fixed hardware scope should be submitted and who decides on it. Where should the project manager direct the team member?`,
  opts:[`To the risk register, which lists the changes the team expects`,
        `To the change management plan, part of the project plan`,
        `To the project charter, which names the sponsor as approver`,
        `To the change log, which records each change request raised`],
  a:1, why:`The change management plan, part of the project management plan, defines how changes are submitted, analyzed and approved, including the change control board. The change log is the near miss. It records each request and its status, but it doesn't define the process or who decides. The charter authorizes the project, not the change process.`,
  task:`R1`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`The PMO requires a 40-page plan template for every project. The project manager is leading a six-week internal project with four team members. What should the project manager do first?`,
  opts:[`Fill in every section of the PMO template`,
        `Skip the template for a short, low risk project`,
        `Propose a tailored version of the template to the PMO`,
        `Ask the sponsor to overrule the PMO for this project`],
  a:2, why:`Tailoring is expected, but within governance, so the PM proposes a right sized version to the PMO that keeps the controls that matter. Filling in every section is the near miss. It follows the rule, but it wastes effort the PMO would likely agree to cut if asked. Skipping it or going around the PMO undermines governance.`,
  task:`R1`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`A two-year project has clear requirements for the first phase but only a broad vision for later phases. How should the project manager approach planning?`,
  opts:[`Plan the first phase in detail and later phases at a high level`,
        `Plan only the first phase and charter later phases separately`,
        `Plan every phase in full detail to complete the baseline now`,
        `Hold off planning until every phase's requirements are approved`],
  a:0, why:`Rolling wave planning is a form of progressive elaboration: detail where you have information, high level placeholders where you don't, refined as you learn. Splitting later phases into separate projects is the near miss. It keeps detail where it's known, but it breaks one business case into pieces nobody manages together. Detailed plans for unknown work are fiction, and waiting stalls the project.`,
  task:`R1`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`An agile team is asked by stakeholders when a set of features will be available. The backlog is estimated in story points and the team has a stable velocity. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Commit to the requested date and ask the team to adjust its pace`,
        `Tell stakeholders that features will arrive when they're ready`,
        `Ask each developer for a completion date and use the latest`,
        `Forecast a date range from the backlog estimates and velocity`],
  a:3, why:`Release planning uses the estimated backlog and the team's proven velocity to forecast a range of dates, then reviews it with stakeholders. Asking each developer is the near miss. It sounds careful, but personal dates ignore the team's measured pace. Committing to the requested date ignores the data, and agile teams can and should forecast.`,
  task:`R1`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`Midway through a sprint, a senior stakeholder asks a developer directly to add a reporting feature before the sprint ends. The developer asks the project manager what to do. What should the project manager advise?`,
  opts:[`Add the feature to the sprint if the work is small`,
        `Refer the stakeholder to the product owner for the backlog`,
        `Tell the stakeholder that scope is frozen for the release`,
        `Swap out the lowest priority story to fit the new feature`],
  a:1, why:`New requests go to the product owner, who owns the backlog and its order, and the sprint goal stays protected. Swapping out a low priority story is the near miss. It sounds balanced, but the developer and PM would be making a priority call that belongs to the product owner. A small add still bypasses them, and freezing all scope isn't agile.`,
  task:`R2`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A deliverable was built exactly to the approved specification, but the customer refuses to accept it because they now expect an additional capability. What should the project manager do first?`,
  opts:[`Add the capability at no cost to close the deliverable`,
        `Ask the sponsor to get the customer to sign acceptance`,
        `Review the acceptance criteria with the customer`,
        `Log a change request for the new capability`],
  a:2, why:`Start by comparing the deliverable with the scope baseline and acceptance criteria together with the customer. A change request is the near miss. It's likely the right path if the capability is out of scope, but it comes after you and the customer agree on what was in scope. Adding it free is gold plating at a loss, and pressing for a signature damages the relationship.`,
  task:`R2`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`The team says it cannot estimate the cost or duration of a deliverable in the work breakdown structure because it is too large and vague. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Decompose the deliverable until each part can be estimated`,
        `Use an analogous estimate from a past project with a buffer`,
        `Remove the deliverable from the WBS until requirements arrive`,
        `Ask the sponsor to approve a fixed amount for the deliverable`],
  a:0, why:`The work package is the lowest WBS level where work can be estimated, scheduled and assigned, so a deliverable that can't be estimated gets decomposed further. An analogous estimate is the near miss. It's a real technique, but here it covers a vague deliverable with a guess instead of breaking it down. Removing scope or fixing an amount doesn't solve the problem.`,
  task:`R2`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`On a hybrid contract, the customer reviews each completed increment at the end of a milestone and signs off before the next payment is released. Which process is this?`,
  opts:[`Quality control, checking deliverables against requirements`,
        `Monitor and control scope, managing changes to the baseline`,
        `Monitor and control project performance against the plan`,
        `Validate scope, the customer's formal acceptance of deliverables`],
  a:3, why:`Validate scope is the customer's or sponsor's formal acceptance of completed deliverables. Quality control is the near miss. It also checks deliverables, but it's the team checking correctness before the customer sees them, and PMBOK 8 places it inside monitor and control scope. Monitor and control scope also manages changes to the baseline, and monitor and control project performance compares results to the plan.`,
  task:`R2`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`A developer added animated dashboards that were not in the requirements because they thought the customer would love them. The extra work delayed testing by two days. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Thank the developer and add the dashboards to the baseline`,
        `Remove the dashboards and log the behavior in the issue log`,
        `Talk with the developer and send the dashboards to change control`,
        `Keep the dashboards and shorten testing to recover the time`],
  a:2, why:`Gold plating, adding features nobody asked for, adds risk, cost and untested scope. The PM talks it through with the developer and lets change control decide on the feature. Removing the dashboards is the near miss. It undoes the gold plating, but it skips the decision and turns a coaching moment into a record against the developer. Adding them to the baseline or cutting testing rewards the problem.`,
  task:`R2`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`During backlog refinement, the team says several user stories at the top of the backlog are too large to finish within one sprint. What should happen next?`,
  opts:[`Extend the next sprint to four weeks to fit the large stories`,
        `Split the stories with the product owner into smaller slices`,
        `Start the large stories and carry the rest into the next sprint`,
        `Ask the team to estimate the stories in more detail first`],
  a:1, why:`Refinement is where large items are split into smaller, valuable, testable slices with the product owner. More detailed estimates are the near miss, but a precise estimate of a story that's too big still leaves it too big. Stretching sprints breaks the cadence, and carrying work over hides real progress.`,
  task:`R2`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A regulator asks how a specific safety requirement was satisfied by the system that was delivered. Which artifact best answers the question?`,
  opts:[`The project's requirements traceability matrix`,
        `The test results log for the final release`,
        `The project charter's high level objectives`,
        `The lessons learned register on safety testing`],
  a:0, why:`The requirements traceability matrix links each requirement to its source, the deliverable that meets it and the tests that verify it, which is the evidence a regulator needs. The test results log is the near miss. It shows tests passed, but it doesn't link them back to this requirement. The charter and lessons register don't show how the requirement was met.`,
  task:`R2`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`At a sprint review, the product owner says a story is not done, but the developers say it meets their standard. The team has never written down what done means. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Back the product owner, who speaks for the customer`,
        `Back the developers, who know the technical work`,
        `Mark the story done and revisit the standard later`,
        `Help the team and PO write a definition of done`],
  a:3, why:`A shared, explicit definition of done prevents this exact disagreement, so the PM facilitates the team and product owner to agree on it and then applies it. Backing the product owner is the near miss. They do accept the work, but without a written standard the same argument returns next sprint. Marking it done ducks the issue.`,
  task:`R2`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`The sponsor wants every planned feature completed before anything is released to users. The product has uncertain market demand. What should the project manager recommend?`,
  opts:[`Release a minimum viable product to learn from real users`,
        `Follow the sponsor's plan to protect the brand`,
        `Run market research first to confirm the features`,
        `Build the features in parallel to release the full set sooner`],
  a:0, why:`With uncertain demand, an MVP tests assumptions early and lets real feedback steer the rest of the investment. Market research is the near miss. It reduces some uncertainty, but it delays the project and tells you what people say, not what they use. Building everything, faster or not, risks features nobody wants.`,
  task:`R3`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A product owner uses weighted shortest job first (cost of delay divided by job size) to sequence work. Feature A has cost of delay 20 and size 5. B has 30 and size 10. C has 12 and size 2. D has 25 and size 8. Which feature goes first?`,
  opts:[`Feature A, with a weighted shortest job first score of 4.0`,
        `Feature B, with the highest cost of delay at 30`,
        `Feature C, with a weighted shortest job first score of 6.0`,
        `Feature D, with a weighted shortest job first score of 3.125`],
  a:2, why:`WSJF equals cost of delay divided by size. A is 20/5 = 4. B is 30/10 = 3. C is 12/2 = 6. D is 25/8 = 3.125. C has the highest score, so it delivers the most value per unit of effort and goes first. B has the biggest cost of delay, but it's also twice the size of A.`,
  task:`R3`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A project's business case promises a 20 percent reduction in claim processing time, which can only be measured six months after the project closes. Who should own measuring that benefit?`,
  opts:[`The project manager, who keeps the project open until it's measured`,
        `The sponsor or business owner, using the benefits management plan`,
        `The project team, reassembled after six months to measure it`,
        `The PMO, which audits closed projects for promised benefits`],
  a:1, why:`Benefits often arrive after closure, so ownership passes to the business owner or sponsor. The project's job is to set up the benefits management plan, the baseline and the measures, then hand them over. The PMO is the near miss. It may track benefits across the portfolio, but the business that gets the benefit is accountable for it. Keeping the project open just to measure wastes money.`,
  task:`R3`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`Usage data shows that customers rarely use most of the features released over the last three sprints. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Keep delivering the approved roadmap as planned`,
        `Ask the team to speed up to ship more features`,
        `Ask marketing to promote the features already released`,
        `Help the PO find out from users why usage is low`],
  a:3, why:`Value based delivery means checking that what you ship is actually valuable, and low usage is feedback. The PM supports the product owner in learning from users and reprioritizing. Promotion is the near miss. It might lift usage, but it assumes users just don't know about the features, before anyone has asked them. Shipping more, or more of the same, repeats the problem.`,
  task:`R3`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A traditional organization plans one large release at the end of an 18-month project. Stakeholders worry about the risk of discovering problems late. What should the project manager propose?`,
  opts:[`Add detailed status reports to show the risk as it builds`,
        `Deliver the product in increments people can use`,
        `Raise the contingency reserve to cover late problems`,
        `Extend the final testing phase to find problems earlier`],
  a:1, why:`Incremental delivery surfaces problems early, delivers value sooner and reduces the risk of one big release. A longer test phase is the near miss. It catches more, but it still finds problems at the end, when they're most expensive. More reporting or reserve treats the symptoms.`,
  task:`R3`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`A team notices that features take 30 days to reach customers, but only 4 of those days involve actual work. What technique best helps the team find and remove the waste?`,
  opts:[`Earned value analysis of the cost of each feature`,
        `Value stream mapping from first request to delivery`,
        `Monte Carlo simulation of the next release date`,
        `A cumulative flow diagram of the team's board`],
  a:1, why:`Value stream mapping shows every step and the waits between them, so the team can see where the 26 idle days go and remove them. The cumulative flow diagram is the near miss. It shows work piling up on the board, but it doesn't cover the steps before and after the board, where much of the waiting often sits. Earned value and Monte Carlo answer cost and date questions.`,
  task:`R3`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`At the end of a sprint, the team plans to show stakeholders a slide summary instead of the working increment because the demo environment is unstable. What should the project manager advise?`,
  opts:[`Present the slides and demo the increment next sprint`,
        `Stabilize the environment to demo the real increment`,
        `Cancel the review and email the slides to stakeholders`,
        `Hold the review with the team only this sprint`],
  a:1, why:`The sprint review exists to inspect the real increment with stakeholders and adapt the backlog from their feedback. Slides now and a demo later is the near miss. It keeps the meeting, but stakeholders can't judge a product they can't see working, and a sprint of feedback is lost. Cancelling or holding it without stakeholders loses it entirely.`,
  task:`R3`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`Halfway through a project, a competitor releases a similar product, and the original business case may no longer hold. What should the project manager do first?`,
  opts:[`Keep delivering to the plan the sponsor approved`,
        `Speed up delivery to beat other competitors to market`,
        `Review the business case with the sponsor`,
        `Ask the product owner to reprioritize the backlog`],
  a:2, why:`When conditions change, the PM reviews the business case with the sponsor, who decides whether to continue, change or stop. Reprioritizing the backlog is the near miss. It may follow, but whether the project still makes sense is the sponsor's decision, and it comes first. Rushing or carrying on assumes an answer nobody has given.`,
  task:`R3`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`In a matrix organization, a functional manager tells the project manager that a key engineer will be moved to another project next week. What should the project manager do first?`,
  opts:[`Escalate to the sponsor and ask them to block the move`,
        `Meet the functional manager to show the project impact`,
        `Plan to spread the engineer's work across the remaining team`,
        `Start finding a contractor to replace the engineer`],
  a:1, why:`In a matrix, resources are negotiated, so the PM talks to the functional manager first, learns their need and shows the project impact to find a solution together. Spreading the work is the near miss. It may be part of the plan, but it accepts the loss before trying to negotiate. Escalating comes only if the talk fails, and a contractor search is premature.`,
  task:`R4`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A resource histogram shows a specialist overallocated in weeks 6 and 7. The end date must not move. Which technique should the project manager use?`,
  opts:[`Resource leveling, delaying work until the specialist is free`,
        `Crashing, adding a second specialist to critical activities`,
        `Resource smoothing, moving work only within available float`,
        `Fast tracking, overlapping the specialist's activities`],
  a:2, why:`Resource smoothing adjusts activities within their float, so the critical path and end date don't move. Leveling is the near miss. It fixes overallocation too, but it can push activities past their float and extend the schedule. Crashing and fast tracking are schedule compression techniques, not resource techniques.`,
  task:`R4`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`On an agile team, a developer asks the project manager to tell them which task to take next. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Assign the highest priority task to the developer`,
        `Ask the product owner to assign the next task`,
        `Tell the developer to pick any task in the sprint`,
        `Encourage them to choose work with the team`],
  a:3, why:`Agile teams self-organize. The servant leader encourages the team to choose work together, guided by the sprint goal and priorities. Assigning the top task is the near miss. It's quick and respects priority, but it builds the habit of waiting to be told. Any task at all ignores the sprint goal, and the product owner doesn't assign tasks.`,
  task:`R4`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A team is split between Denver and a contractor group eight hours ahead. The remote group always joins meetings late in their evening and engagement is dropping. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Set team working agreements on rotating times and async updates`,
        `Ask the remote group to shift its hours to match Denver`,
        `Replace live meetings with email updates and recorded demos`,
        `Raise the engagement drop with the contractor's management`],
  a:0, why:`Virtual teams work when the whole team sets fair working agreements: shared overlap, rotating inconvenience and asynchronous updates. Moving everything to email is the near miss. It ends the odd hours, but it removes the live contact a split team needs. Shifting the burden to one group erodes trust, and going to their management treats a scheduling problem as a conduct one.`,
  task:`R4`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`While reviewing the RACI chart, the project manager notices that two people are marked Accountable for the same deliverable. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Keep both as Accountable for added oversight`,
        `Change both to Responsible for the deliverable`,
        `Name one person as the single Accountable owner`,
        `Ask the two people to agree on who should be Accountable`],
  a:2, why:`Each deliverable has exactly one Accountable person. Two creates confusion over who owns the outcome, and the other person can be Responsible, Consulted or Informed. Letting the two decide is the near miss. They may agree, but assigning accountability is the PM's call, and it shouldn't wait on a negotiation. Making both Responsible leaves nobody Accountable.`,
  task:`R4`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A task requires 120 hours of effort. Two engineers are assigned, and each has 6 productive hours per day available for it. How many working days will the task take?`,
  opts:[`10 working days for the task`,
        `20 working days for the task`,
        `15 working days for the task`,
        `12 working days for the task`],
  a:0, why:`Daily capacity is 2 engineers times 6 hours, or 12 hours per day. Duration is 120 hours divided by 12 hours per day, which equals 10 working days.`,
  task:`R4`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A new team member lacks experience with a tool that the project depends on. The work starts in three weeks. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Replace the member with someone who knows the tool`,
        `Plan targeted training for the member now`,
        `Give the tool work to the most experienced person`,
        `Ask the member to learn the tool on their own time`],
  a:1, why:`Developing team skills is part of managing resources, and targeted training with pairing builds capability and spreads knowledge in the three weeks available. Routing the work to the expert is the near miss. It's safe this month, but it creates a bottleneck and leaves the new member behind. Replacing them or leaving it to their own time skips the PM's role.`,
  task:`R4`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`A buyer needs a vendor to start a small piece of work immediately, but the scope cannot be defined in detail yet. Which contract type best fits?`,
  opts:[`Firm fixed price, setting the total cost up front`,
        `Fixed price with economic price adjustment, tied to an index`,
        `Cost plus fixed fee, with costs reimbursed as they occur`,
        `Time and materials, with a not-to-exceed limit`],
  a:3, why:`When scope is unclear and work must start right away, time and materials fits, ideally with a not-to-exceed cap to protect the buyer. Cost plus fixed fee is the near miss. It also handles unclear scope, but it's built for larger, high risk work like research, where the buyer reimburses audited seller costs. Fixed price contracts need well defined scope.`,
  task:`R5`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A fixed price incentive fee contract has a target cost of $200,000, a target fee of $20,000, a buyer/seller share ratio of 70/30, and a ceiling price of $240,000. The seller's actual cost is $180,000. What is the final price the buyer pays?`,
  opts:[`$200,000 final price`,
        `$206,000 final price`,
        `$220,000 final price`,
        `$214,000 final price`],
  a:1, why:`The underrun is $200,000 minus $180,000, or $20,000. The seller keeps 30 percent of it, $6,000, so the fee becomes $20,000 plus $6,000, or $26,000. Final price is $180,000 plus $26,000, or $206,000, which is below the $240,000 ceiling.`,
  task:`R5`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A cost plus fixed fee contract has an estimated cost of $500,000 and a fixed fee of $40,000. The seller's actual allowable cost is $560,000. How much does the buyer pay in total?`,
  opts:[`$540,000 in total`,
        `$560,000 in total`,
        `$600,000 in total`,
        `$580,000 in total`],
  a:2, why:`The fee on this contract was set when it was signed, so it doesn't move. The buyer covers the $560,000 the seller actually spent and adds the $40,000 fee: $600,000. An overrun costs the buyer dollar for dollar.`,
  task:`R5`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`Your vendor works under cost plus incentive fee terms: target cost $300,000, target fee $24,000, share ratio 80/20 (buyer/seller). The vendor ends up spending $340,000. What does the buyer pay in total?`,
  opts:[`$364,000`,
        `$348,000`,
        `$356,000`,
        `$332,000`],
  a:2, why:`The vendor went $40,000 over its $300,000 target. The seller eats 20 percent of that, $8,000, so its $24,000 fee shrinks to $16,000. Costs get reimbursed in full, so it's $340,000 plus $16,000: $356,000.`,
  task:`R5`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`In the clinic case, how should the project manager handle the vendor's failed release?`,
  opts:[`Accept the release and log the four failures as defects`,
        `Reject it in writing and invoke the 10 day fix clause`,
        `Have the internal team fix the four failures this sprint`,
        `Withhold the next payment until the release is fixed`],
  a:1, why:`The release didn't meet its acceptance criteria, so it isn't accepted. Tell the vendor in writing which tests failed and hold them to the contract's remedy, a fix within 10 business days at their cost. Withholding payment is the near miss. It may be allowed, but only if the contract provides for it, and it comes after the agreed remedy fails. Accepting it with defects gives up the buyer's leverage, and fixing it in house pays twice for the same work.`,
  task:`R5`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive`, case:`m1c1`, cq:2 },

{ q:`A team can lease a machine for $1,200 per day, or buy it for $24,000 and pay $400 per day to operate it. After how many days does buying become the cheaper option?`,
  opts:[`After 20 days of use`,
        `After 15 days of use`,
        `After 60 days of use`,
        `After 30 days of use`],
  a:3, why:`Buying saves $1,200 minus $400, or $800 per day. Breakeven is $24,000 divided by $800 per day, which is 30 days. Beyond 30 days of use, buying costs less than leasing.`,
  task:`R5`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A buyer is hiring a vendor to build software with an agile approach, and the scope will evolve sprint by sprint. Which contract structure best supports this?`,
  opts:[`A firm fixed price for the full scope agreed upfront`,
        `A cost plus award fee based on a final customer rating`,
        `A fixed price per sprint with flexible scope`,
        `A time and materials contract with no cap on hours`],
  a:2, why:`Agile friendly contracts fix price or capacity per increment and let scope flex through backlog changes, which balances buyer control with changing requirements. Uncapped time and materials is the near miss. It lets scope flex, but it leaves the buyer with all the cost risk. A firm price for the full scope assumes requirements that don't exist yet.`,
  task:`R5`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`The chart shows planned value, earned value, and actual cost through month 5 of an 8 month project. What is the project's status at month 5?`,
  fig:`<prop type="chart" kind="evm" title="Earned value, $ thousands, months 1 to 8 (status date: end of month 5)" pv="50,120,210,300,380,450,510,560" ev="45,100,170,240,300" ac="50,115,200,290,370">At month 5: PV 380, EV 300, AC 370.</prop>`,
  opts:[`Ahead of schedule and under budget`,
        `Behind schedule and over budget`,
        `Behind schedule and under budget`,
        `Ahead of schedule and over budget`],
  a:1, why:`At month 5 earned value ($300,000) sits below both planned value ($380,000) and actual cost ($370,000). EV below PV means behind schedule, SPI = 300 / 380 = 0.79. EV below AC means over budget, CPI = 300 / 370 = 0.81. Behind and under budget is the near miss: it comes from comparing AC with PV instead of with EV. The two ahead answers read the curves the wrong way round.`,
  task:`R6`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`In the bridge case, what is the estimate at completion if cost performance continues as it has?`,
  opts:[`$12.0 million`,
        `$12.6 million`,
        `$13.3 million`,
        `$10.8 million`],
  a:2, why:`CPI = EV / AC = 5.4 / 6.0 = 0.90. When current performance is expected to continue, EAC = BAC / CPI = 12.0 / 0.90 = $13.3 million. $12.6 million is the near miss: it's AC + (BAC minus EV), which assumes the rest of the work goes to plan, and the labor rates will stay high. $12.0 million ignores the variance, and $10.8 million multiplies by the CPI instead of dividing.`,
  task:`R6`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive`, case:`m1c2`, cq:1 },

{ q:`Same project: budget at completion $500,000, earned value $200,000, actual cost $250,000. The overrun came from a one-time vendor error, and future work should go as planned. What is the estimate at completion?`,
  opts:[`$625,000 estimate at completion`,
        `$500,000 estimate at completion`,
        `$600,000 estimate at completion`,
        `$550,000 estimate at completion`],
  a:3, why:`When the variance is atypical, remaining work is expected at budget: EAC = AC + (BAC minus EV) = 250,000 + (500,000 minus 200,000) = $550,000.`,
  task:`R6`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A project has a budget at completion of $500,000, earned value of $200,000, and actual cost of $250,000. What cost performance must the team achieve on the remaining work to finish within the original budget?`,
  opts:[`A TCPI of 1.20 on the remaining work`,
        `A TCPI of 0.80 on the remaining work`,
        `A TCPI of 1.25 on the remaining work`,
        `A TCPI of 1.00 on the remaining work`],
  a:0, why:`TCPI based on BAC = (BAC minus EV) / (BAC minus AC) = (500,000 minus 200,000) / (500,000 minus 250,000) = 300,000 / 250,000 = 1.20. The team must get $1.20 of value for every dollar spent from here on.`,
  task:`R6`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`On a hybrid project, a risk that was identified and analyzed during release planning has now occurred. The response will cost $15,000. Where should the money come from?`,
  opts:[`The management reserve, with sponsor approval`,
        `The contingency reserve inside the cost baseline`,
        `A change request raising the budget by $15,000`,
        `The operating budget of the department that raised it`],
  a:1, why:`Contingency reserves cover known risks, the known unknowns, and sit inside the cost baseline for the PM to use. The management reserve is the near miss, but it's for unknown unknowns and needs management approval. A change request to raise the budget isn't needed when the reserve already covers this.`,
  task:`R6`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`Work package estimates total $800,000. The contingency reserve is $80,000 and the management reserve is $50,000. What are the cost baseline and the total project budget?`,
  opts:[`Baseline $800,000 and budget $930,000`,
        `Baseline $930,000 and budget $930,000`,
        `Baseline $850,000 and budget $930,000`,
        `Baseline $880,000 and budget $930,000`],
  a:3, why:`The cost baseline includes work package estimates plus contingency: 800,000 + 80,000 = $880,000. The project budget adds the management reserve: 880,000 + 50,000 = $930,000.`,
  task:`R6`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`An agile team costs $40,000 per two-week sprint, including all members and tools. The product owner estimates six more sprints to reach the release goal. What is the forecast cost to complete?`,
  opts:[`$200,000 to complete`,
        `$240,000 to complete`,
        `$280,000 to complete`,
        `$120,000 to complete`],
  a:1, why:`Agile teams are often funded by burn rate. Forecast cost equals cost per sprint times remaining sprints: $40,000 times 6 = $240,000.`,
  task:`R6`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`The cost performance index has dropped from 0.98 to 0.86 over three reporting periods. What should the project manager do next?`,
  opts:[`Wait one more period to confirm the trend`,
        `Ask the sponsor for funding to cover the overrun`,
        `Reduce testing scope to bring spending back in line`,
        `Analyze the variance to find the cause of the drop`],
  a:3, why:`Three periods of decline is a trend, so the PM analyzes the cause, updates the forecast and reports with options. Asking for funding is the near miss. It may be one of the options, but it comes after the cause is known, since the fix might be elsewhere. Waiting hides the problem, and cutting testing trades cost for quality without a decision.`,
  task:`R6`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`In the bridge case, in cost of quality terms, what is the cost of grinding out and rewelding the two welds?`,
  opts:[`A prevention cost`,
        `An appraisal cost`,
        `An internal failure cost`,
        `An external failure cost`],
  a:2, why:`Rework on defects found before the product reaches the customer is an internal failure cost, part of the cost of nonconformance. The reinspection is an appraisal cost, which makes appraisal the near miss. Prevention covers things like training and design reviews, and external failure costs come from defects the customer finds after delivery.`,
  task:`R7`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive`, case:`m1c2`, cq:4 },

{ q:`An auditor reviews whether the team is following its defined testing processes and recommends improvements. Which activity is this?`,
  opts:[`Manage quality assurance, which looks at the process`,
        `Quality control, which inspects the deliverables`,
        `Validate scope, where the customer accepts deliverables`,
        `Quality planning, which sets the quality standards`],
  a:0, why:`Manage quality assurance, as PMBOK 8 names it, focuses on whether the team follows its processes and includes quality audits. Quality control is the near miss. It's also about quality, but it inspects the products, not the process. Quality planning sets the standards, and validate scope is customer acceptance.`,
  task:`R7`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`The control chart shows coating thickness for the last 20 samples. What should the project manager do?`,
  fig:`<prop type="chart" kind="control" title="Coating thickness, microns, last 20 samples" mean="50" ucl="56" lcl="44" values="49,51,50,48,52,50,49,51,53,50,48,51,58,50,49,52,50,51,48,50"></prop>`,
  opts:[`Have the team look for an assignable cause for sample 13`,
        `Treat sample 13 as normal variation in the process`,
        `Recalculate the control limits to include sample 13`,
        `Reject every batch coated after the chart started`],
  a:0, why:`Sample 13 sits above the upper control limit while the rest vary randomly around the mean, a sign that something specific changed. Look for the assignable cause and act on it. Treating it as normal variation is the near miss. Variation inside the limits is normal, but a point outside them is the signal the chart exists to catch. Moving the limits hides it, and rejecting everything comes before anyone knows the cause.`,
  task:`R7`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`An agile team keeps finding many defects in a testing phase at the end of each release. What should the team do?`,
  opts:[`Add a longer hardening phase before each release`,
        `Have a separate test team inspect each finished story`,
        `Build testing into each sprint's definition of done`,
        `Accept minor defects to protect the release date`],
  a:2, why:`Quality is built in, not inspected in. Testing inside each sprint, backed by the definition of done, catches defects when they're cheapest to fix. A separate test team is the near miss. It tests sooner, but it keeps testing as a handoff after the work instead of part of it. A longer hardening phase keeps the late feedback loop.`,
  task:`R7`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A project has hundreds of defect reports from many causes. The team has limited time and wants to fix the causes that matter most. Which tool should the project manager use?`,
  opts:[`A Pareto chart, ranking causes by how many defects each one produces`,
        `A scatter diagram, comparing two variables to find a correlation`,
        `A flowchart, mapping the steps of the process from start to end`,
        `A histogram, showing the distribution of a single measured variable`],
  a:0, why:`A Pareto chart ranks causes by frequency so the team can attack the vital few that cause most defects, the 80/20 principle.`,
  task:`R7`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`An agile team building a mobile app learns the company has set energy use targets for software running on its servers. The team's definition of done covers tests and code review only. What should the team do?`,
  opts:[`Measure energy use once a year in a separate audit`,
        `Leave energy to the operations team that runs servers`,
        `Add an energy story to the backlog for a later release`,
        `Add an energy check to the definition of done`],
  a:3, why:`Build sustainability into quality the same way as any other quality criterion: an agreed energy check in the definition of done applies to every increment. A later backlog story is the near miss. It gets the work planned, but every increment until then ships unchecked. A yearly audit finds problems long after they're built in, and operations can't fix code it didn't write.`,
  task:`R7`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A supplier offers a cheaper packaging material that passes every test, but it can't be recycled and the customer pays to dispose of it. The quality plan includes the customer's sustainability criteria. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Choose the cheaper material that passes the tests`,
        `Ask the customer to accept the cost of disposal`,
        `Compare the life cycle cost of both materials`,
        `Have the supplier certify the material as recyclable`],
  a:2, why:`Cost of quality and sustainability look past the purchase price to the life cycle cost, including disposal, and to the criteria the customer set. Compare both materials on that basis before deciding. Passing the tests is the near miss. It covers performance, but not the sustainability criteria in the plan. Asking the customer to absorb the cost only moves it, and no certificate makes the material recyclable.`,
  task:`R7`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`An activity has an optimistic estimate of 4 days, a most likely estimate of 7 days, and a pessimistic estimate of 16 days. Using the beta (PERT) distribution, what are the expected duration and standard deviation?`,
  opts:[`Expected 8 days, standard deviation 2 days`,
        `Expected 9 days, standard deviation 2 days`,
        `Expected 7 days, standard deviation 3 days`,
        `Expected 8 days, standard deviation 3 days`],
  a:0, why:`Expected = (O + 4M + P) / 6 = (4 + 28 + 16) / 6 = 48 / 6 = 8 days. Standard deviation = (P minus O) / 6 = (16 minus 4) / 6 = 2 days. PMBOK 8 shows the triangular formula, (O + M + P) / 3, which gives 9 days here, so check which one the question asks for.`,
  task:`R8`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`Using the network diagram, what is the critical path and its duration?`,
  fig:`<prop type="chart" kind="network" title="Network diagram, durations in days" nodes="A:3,B:5,C:4,D:2,E:6,F:3" edges="A>B,A>C,B>D,C>E,D>F,E>F"></prop>`,
  opts:[`A, B, D, F at 13 days`,
        `A, C, E at 13 days`,
        `A, B, D, F at 16 days`,
        `A, C, E, F at 16 days`],
  a:3, why:`Add each path. A, B, D, F is 3 + 5 + 2 + 3 = 13 days, and A, C, E, F is 3 + 4 + 6 + 3 = 16 days. The longest path is the critical path, so A, C, E, F at 16 days, and B and D have 3 days of float. A, B, D, F at 16 days is the near miss: the right duration on the wrong path. A, C, E leaves out F, which both paths end on.`,
  task:`R8`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A project is three weeks behind schedule. The sponsor will not approve any additional cost but accepts more risk. Which compression technique should the project manager use?`,
  opts:[`Crashing, adding resources to critical path work`,
        `Resource leveling, smoothing out overallocation`,
        `Fast tracking, overlapping critical path activities`,
        `A deadline extension, approved through change control`],
  a:2, why:`Fast tracking overlaps activities that would normally run in sequence. It adds risk and rework, not direct cost. Crashing is the near miss, since it also shortens the critical path, but adding resources costs money the sponsor won't approve. Leveling usually lengthens the schedule, and an extension doesn't recover the three weeks.`,
  task:`R8`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`In the clinic case, what should the project manager do about the integration forecast?`,
  opts:[`Agree with the product owner what fits before wave 1`,
        `Ask the team to raise its velocity to 50 a sprint`,
        `Move wave 1 back four weeks to fit all 150 points`,
        `Keep the date and report any gap after sprint 9`],
  a:0, why:`At about 30 points a sprint, 150 points takes five sprints, or ten weeks, and wave 1 is six weeks away, so about 90 points fit. The product owner and sponsor decide what wave 1 needs most, or whether the date moves, with that forecast in front of them. Moving wave 1 is the near miss. It may be the answer, but it isn't the PM's call alone, and it may not be needed if wave 1 needs less. Asking for a higher velocity overcommits the team, and waiting until sprint 9 hides a gap the data already shows.`,
  task:`R8`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile`, case:`m1c1`, cq:1 },

{ q:`After a concrete foundation is poured, it must cure for five days before framing can begin. How should this be represented in the schedule?`,
  opts:[`As a five-day lead on the framing activity`,
        `As a five-day lag between pouring and framing`,
        `As five days of free float on the pouring activity`,
        `As a five-day buffer at the end of the project`],
  a:1, why:`A lag is a required waiting time between a predecessor and successor. Curing time is a classic finish-to-start lag. A lead would let framing start early, the opposite.`,
  task:`R8`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`A project must be shortened by one day. Activity A is critical and costs $2,000 per day to crash. B is critical and costs $1,500 per day. C is not critical and costs $800 per day. Which activity should be crashed?`,
  opts:[`Activity A, the first critical activity`,
        `Activity C, the lowest crash cost overall`,
        `Activities A and B, one day each`,
        `Activity B, the cheapest critical activity`],
  a:3, why:`Only critical path activities shorten the project, and of those you crash the cheapest per day, activity B at $1,500. C is the near miss on price, but it isn't critical, so crashing it spends $800 without moving the end date. Crashing both A and B spends $3,500 for a one day need.`,
  task:`R8`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`An agile team has missed its sprint commitment four sprints in a row. Management wants the team to commit to more points to catch up. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Agree with management and ask for extra hours next sprint`,
        `Plan the next sprint on the team's actual velocity`,
        `Lengthen the sprints to meet the original commitments`,
        `Re-estimate the backlog with the team before the next sprint`],
  a:1, why:`Plan to proven velocity, not hope, and look for the cause of the spillover, such as large stories or interruptions. Re-estimating is the near miss. Estimates may be off, but new estimates without a change in how much the team plans to take on lead to the same misses. Committing to more or working overtime makes it worse.`,
  task:`R8`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A hybrid project has a fixed regulatory submission date and several agile software teams. How should the project manager manage the schedule?`,
  opts:[`Track only the regulatory date in a detailed Gantt chart`,
        `Let teams sprint independently and check readiness near the date`,
        `Convert the software work into fixed tasks on a detailed schedule`,
        `Map the team sprint cadences to a milestone schedule`],
  a:3, why:`A milestone schedule anchored on the fixed date, with team sprints mapped to the milestones, gives one integrated view while the teams work iteratively. Independent sprints with a late check is the near miss. It respects agile, but a readiness check close to the date leaves no time to react. Converting everything to fixed tasks fights the approach.`,
  task:`R8`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`The chart shows a team's sprint burndown through day 7 of a 10 day sprint. What should the project manager do?`,
  fig:`<prop type="chart" title="Sprint burndown, points remaining (10 day sprint, 40 points planned)" labels="Day 1,Day 2,Day 3,Day 4,Day 5,Day 6,Day 7" values="40,37,34,34,34,34,33"></prop>`,
  opts:[`Report to the sponsor that the sprint has failed`,
        `Raise the stall with the team at the next standup`,
        `Ask each developer to log hours on every task`,
        `Wait for the sprint review to discuss the trend`],
  a:1, why:`The burndown has been flat since day 3, with 33 of 40 points left and three days to go, so something is blocking the work. Raise it with the team at the daily standup to find the impediment, then work with the product owner on what can still meet the sprint goal. Waiting for the review is the near miss. The review will show it, but by then the sprint is over. Declaring failure to the sponsor skips the team, and logging hours measures effort instead of finding the block.`,
  task:`R9`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`An AI tool turns the team's sprint data into a status summary for stakeholders. It says the release is on track, but the burnup shows scope growing faster than completed work. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Send the summary built from the team's own data`,
        `Send both and let stakeholders decide which is right`,
        `Check the summary against the data before sending`,
        `Stop using the tool and write every report by hand`],
  a:2, why:`AI output is a draft, and the project manager is accountable for what goes out. Check it against the source data and correct it before stakeholders see it. Here the burnup contradicts the summary. Sending both is the near miss. It's transparent, but it hands stakeholders a contradiction the PM should have resolved. Sending the summary as is reports a status that isn't true, and dropping the tool throws away its help instead of reviewing it.`,
  task:`R9`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`On a hybrid program, the executive steering committee asks for a monthly status report. What should the report emphasize?`,
  opts:[`A short summary of forecasts and the decisions needed`,
        `A full list of every task the team finished this month`,
        `Detailed technical notes on the engineering work`,
        `Velocity charts and story counts for each team`],
  a:0, why:`Executives need status in context: variances, trends, forecasts and the decisions they have to make. Velocity charts are the near miss. They're real delivery data, but they're team level detail that doesn't tell executives what to decide. Task lists and technical notes miss the audience.`,
  task:`R9`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`At a status date, earned value is $45,000 and planned value is $50,000. What is the schedule variance, and what does it mean?`,
  opts:[`Positive $5,000, meaning ahead of schedule`,
        `0.90, meaning 10 percent behind on cost`,
        `Negative $5,000, meaning behind schedule`,
        `Negative $5,000, meaning over budget`],
  a:2, why:`SV = EV minus PV = 45,000 minus 50,000 = negative $5,000. A negative schedule variance means less work has been done than planned, so the project is behind schedule. It says nothing about cost, which is why the over budget answer is wrong. 0.90 is the SPI, not the SV.`,
  task:`R9`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`What does the cumulative flow diagram show?`,
  fig:`<prop type="chart" kind="cfd" title="Cumulative flow, items by state, weeks 1 to 8" labels="W1,W2,W3,W4,W5,W6,W7,W8" done="2,5,8,10,11,12,12,13" testing="3,4,5,7,10,13,16,18" doing="5,7,7,7,6,5,4,3" backlog="30,24,21,17,14,12,10,8"></prop>`,
  opts:[`Scope is growing faster than the team can deliver`,
        `Work is piling up in the testing column`,
        `The team is finishing more work every week`,
        `Work in progress is stable and flow is healthy`],
  a:1, why:`In a cumulative flow diagram each band's thickness is the work sitting in that state. The testing band widens every week while done barely rises, so items enter testing faster than they leave: a bottleneck. The team should limit work entering testing and help clear it. Growing scope is the near miss, but that shows as the top line climbing, and here it's nearly flat. Done is flattening, not rising faster, and a widening band is the opposite of stable flow.`,
  task:`R9`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`Every team lead reports green status, but the project manager's data shows key milestones slipping and defects rising. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Report green, trusting the leads who are closest to the work`,
        `Report red to the sponsor before talking to the leads`,
        `Ask the leads to report amber as a middle ground`,
        `Review the data with the leads to agree on the real status`],
  a:3, why:`This is a watermelon project, green outside and red inside. The PM reconciles the reports with the objective data alongside the leads, then reports the true status. Reporting red right away is the near miss. It's honest, but it skips the leads, who may know something the data doesn't, and it costs their trust. Green or amber misreports the facts.`,
  task:`R9`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`The project management plan says any cost performance index below 0.90 must be escalated to the steering committee. The current CPI is 0.88. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Escalate to the steering committee with options`,
        `Wait a period to see whether the CPI recovers`,
        `Escalate only if the CPI falls further below 0.90`,
        `Propose a lower threshold through change control`],
  a:0, why:`Variance thresholds exist to get decisions to the right level on time. The PM follows the plan and escalates with analysis and recommended options. Waiting a period is the near miss. It seems prudent, but the plan already set the line, and 0.88 is past it. Lowering the threshold to avoid escalating defeats its purpose.`,
  task:`R9`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`On a release burnup chart, the completed work line rises steadily, but the total scope line has risen in each of the last four sprints. What does this show?`,
  opts:[`The team's velocity is falling sprint over sprint`,
        `Added scope is pushing out the completion forecast`,
        `The team is ahead of plan and will finish early`,
        `The chart is set up wrong for a burnup`],
  a:1, why:`A burnup shows completed work and total scope as separate lines. A rising scope line means work is being added, which moves the forecast completion date. That's a conversation for the product owner and stakeholders. Falling velocity is the near miss, but the completed line is rising steadily, so the pace is fine.`,
  task:`R9`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`The sponsor cancels a project halfway through because the business strategy changed. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Stop all work and release the team right away`,
        `Run the standard closure process for the project`,
        `Ask the sponsor to reconsider before the team stops`,
        `Finish the deliverables already in progress`],
  a:1, why:`Terminated projects still go through closure: document what was done, archive records, close contracts, release resources and capture lessons learned. Stopping and releasing the team is the near miss. Work does stop, but releasing people before closure loses the records and lessons. Finishing deliverables or pushing back ignores the sponsor's decision.`,
  task:`R10`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`All work on a hybrid project is complete, including the final software increment. Before the project manager archives the project records and releases the team, what must happen?`,
  opts:[`The lessons learned session must be held with the sponsor only`,
        `The team must be reassigned by the functional managers first`,
        `The customer must formally accept the final deliverables`,
        `The final invoices must be paid to every vendor`],
  a:2, why:`Formal acceptance of the final deliverables comes before administrative closure, and without it the project isn't complete. Paying vendors is the near miss. Contract closure is part of closing, but it doesn't confirm the customer accepted the work. Lessons learned involve more than the sponsor, and reassignment comes after the records are archived.`,
  task:`R10`, domain:`Process`, approach:`hybrid` },

{ q:`An agile product is moving to a support team after its final release. The team says it already ran retrospectives every sprint, so a closing lessons learned review is a waste of time. What should the project manager do?`,
  opts:[`Skip the review and rely on the sprint retrospectives`,
        `Hold a short closing review that rolls up the retro themes`,
        `Ask each member to email their top lessons before leaving`,
        `Leave lessons learned to the support team later`],
  a:1, why:`Retrospectives improve the team's own process sprint to sprint. Closure is where the project rolls those themes up into the organization's process assets so other teams benefit. Emailed lessons are the near miss. They collect something, but scattered emails don't get pulled together or shared. Skipping it or leaving it to support loses it.`,
  task:`R10`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`During procurement closure, the vendor has an open claim for additional payment that the buyer disputes. What should happen before the contract is closed?`,
  opts:[`Close the contract and pass the claim to the legal team`,
        `Pay the claim in full to close the contract on time`,
        `Hold the final payment until the vendor drops the claim`,
        `Settle the claim through negotiation or the dispute process`],
  a:3, why:`Claims are settled before the contract closes, preferably by negotiation, otherwise through the dispute process the contract defines. Passing it to legal is the near miss. Legal may well be involved, but closing first leaves an open obligation on a closed contract. Paying in full just to close skips the process, and withholding payment to force the issue is bad faith.`,
  task:`R10`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` },

{ q:`An agile team is about to hand a released product to an operations team that will support it. What best ensures a smooth transition?`,
  opts:[`Hand over the source code for operations to learn as issues arise`,
        `Keep the development team on call for operations indefinitely`,
        `Train operations and agree on runbooks before the handoff`,
        `Hold the release until operations reviews every feature`],
  a:2, why:`Transition is planned, not improvised: involve operations early, transfer knowledge through runbooks and training, and confirm support readiness before handover. Keeping developers on call is the near miss. Some early support is wise, but indefinite on call means the transfer never really happens. Learning from issues and holding the release both leave operations unprepared.`,
  task:`R10`, domain:`Process`, approach:`agile` },

{ q:`A project is in closing, and the team members are returning to their functional managers. What should the project manager do as part of releasing them?`,
  opts:[`Release everyone on the same day once the work ends`,
        `Give each functional manager input on their people`,
        `Keep the team on the budget until the portfolio review`,
        `Ask each member to draft their own performance review`],
  a:1, why:`Releasing resources is part of closure. Functional managers usually own the performance review, so specific input on each person's contributions makes sure the project work counts. Releasing everyone at once is the near miss. People do go back, but a single day ignores handoffs and leaves no input for their managers. Holding people on the budget wastes money, and a self review isn't feedback.`,
  task:`R10`, domain:`Process`, approach:`predictive` }

]});
